## Impact

## Fix

## Resolution
Added decodePinboardResult, which both updateBookmark and deleteBookmark now call after the status check; anything other than result_code "done" is an error. format=json is set centrally by pinboardGet so the body is parseable. Covered by TestUpdateBookmark_RejectsInBandFailure ("something went wrong" at HTTP 200), TestDeleteBookmark/item_not_found, TestApplyUpdateAction_CountsFailureWhenNotVerbose, TestApplyDeleteAction_CountsFailure.

## Problem

`collectTagsFromSuggestions` appends `.autoTagged` outside the loop and outside the `len(tags) < 10` guard. Two consequences:

1. When Pinboard returns no suggestions, the function still returns `[".autoTagged"]`. `getSuggestedTags` then returns a non-empty string, `fetchTagsIfNeeded` treats it as a successful fetch, and `shouldUpdateBookmark` returns true — so the bookmark is rewritten with a single marker tag and no real content.
2. The advertised cap is 10, but the cap is applied before the append, so the result is 11 tags.

Because the rewrite goes through `posts/add`, each of these no-value updates carries the metadata destruction described in the `updateBookmark` finding: creation date reset, `toread` cleared, `shared` re-defaulted.</problem>
<parameter name="evidence">main.go:559-585 — the append is unconditional:

```go
	if len(suggestions) > 0 {
		for _, tag := range suggestions[0].Popular {
			if !tagMap[tag] && len(tags) < 10 { ... }
		}
		for _, tag := range suggestions[0].Recommended {
			if !tagMap[tag] && len(tags) < 10 { ... }
		}
	}

	// Always add .autoTagged tag
	tags = append(tags, ".autoTagged")

	return tags
```

main.go:203-214 — a lone `.autoTagged` counts as a successful fetch:

```go
fetchedTags, err := getSuggestedTags(apiToken, urlToFetch)
stats.RecordTagFetch(err == nil && fetchedTags != "")

if err == nil && fetchedTags != "" {
	...
	return fetchedTags
}
```

main.go:161-167 — any non-empty tag string forces an update:

```go
tagsAdded := newTags != ""

return urlChanged || titleAdded || tagsAdded
```

The existing tests codify both behaviours rather than catching them. main_test.go:248 asserts the empty-suggestion case *should* return the marker:

```go
	// Should only have .autoTagged when no suggestions
	if tags != ".autoTagged" {
```

and main_test.go:274 asserts the off-by-one cap:

```go
	if len(tagList) != 11 { // 10 + .autoTagged
```

README.md:20 states the contract the code does not meet:

> **Auto-Tagging**: Adds up to 10 suggested tags (from Pinboard API) for untagged bookmarks

Pinboard API documentation, Data Types, notes an effect the README never mentions:

> Please be aware that tags beginning with a period are treated as private and trigger special private tag semantics.</evidence>
<parameter name="impact">Every untagged bookmark for which Pinboard has no suggestions — typically the obscure and long-tail URLs — is rewritten to gain a single marker tag it derives no benefit from, at the cost of a rate-limited write and its original creation date, unread flag, and privacy default. On an account with many untagged bookmarks this converts a read-mostly maintenance pass into a bulk rewrite. The tag count also exceeds the documented maximum by one.</impact>
<parameter name="fix">Only mark bookmarks that actually received tags, and count the marker against the cap:

```go
// collectTagsFromSuggestions returns at most 10 suggested tags plus the
// .autoTagged marker. It returns nil when Pinboard suggested nothing:
// returning a bare marker would make shouldUpdateBookmark rewrite a
// bookmark that gained no tags, resetting its creation date via posts/add.
func collectTagsFromSuggestions(...) []string {
	...
	if len(tags) == 0 {
		return nil
	}

	return append(tags, ".autoTagged")
}
```

Change the cap to `len(tags) < 9` so the marker brings the total to the documented 10, or update README.md:20 to say 11 — either is defensible, but the code and the doc must agree.

Update `TestGetSuggestedTags_EmptyResponse` to assert an empty string and `TestGetSuggestedTags_MaxTenTags` to assert the chosen total. Both currently lock in the behaviour being fixed.

Separately, document in README.md that `.autoTagged` begins with a period and therefore triggers Pinboard's private-tag semantics — users adopting this tool should know it changes how those bookmarks are treated.</fix>
</invoke>

## Evidence

## Impact

## Fix

## Resolution
collectTagsFromSuggestions returns nil when Pinboard suggested nothing, so an untagged bookmark with no suggestions is no longer rewritten to gain a bare marker. The cap is now maxSuggestedTags (9) plus the marker, giving the ten tags README documents. Covered by TestCollectTagsFromSuggestions, TestGetSuggestedTags ("returns nothing when there are no suggestions"), and TestGetSuggestedTags_CapsTagCount, which asserts the total and that the marker is last. README now states the 9+1 split and that a leading period makes .autoTagged a Pinboard private tag.

## Problem

`expandAndCheckURL` validates after each of its three transformation steps, whether or not the step changed anything. For the overwhelmingly common bookmark — not a shortener, no trailing parenthesis, no redirect — all three transformations return the input unchanged, so `validateURLAccessibility` performs three identical `HEAD` requests to the same URL.

`urlRedirects` adds a fourth `HEAD` to the same URL on top of that.</problem>
<parameter name="evidence">main.go:587-616:

```go
expandedURL, err := expandURL(url, verbose)
...
if err := validateURLAccessibility(expandedURL, "expanded URL"); err != nil { return "", err }

fixedURL, err := fixParenthesesSuffix(expandedURL, verbose)
...
if err := validateURLAccessibility(fixedURL, "fixed URL"); err != nil { return "", err }

redirectedURL, err := urlRedirects(fixedURL, verbose)
...
if err := validateURLAccessibility(redirectedURL, "redirected URL"); err != nil { return "", err }
```

For a plain URL, `expandURL` returns it unchanged (main.go:735-737, the `default` branch), `fixParenthesesSuffix` returns early unchanged (main.go:641-643), and `urlRedirects` returns it unchanged (main.go:705). So `expandedURL == fixedURL == redirectedURL == url`, and the three `validateURLAccessibility` calls are byte-identical.

Counting requests for one unremarkable bookmark:

```
HEAD url   validateURLAccessibility("expanded URL")
HEAD url   validateURLAccessibility("fixed URL")
HEAD url   urlRedirects
HEAD url   validateURLAccessibility("redirected URL")
```

Four requests where one suffices. The stats confirm the triple-count is recorded as real data — `validateURLAccessibility` calls `stats.RecordHTTPStatus("url-validation", ...)` on every invocation (main.go:630), so the "URL Validation" block in the statistics report shows roughly 3x the true number of checked bookmarks.</evidence>
<parameter name="impact">Roughly a 4x multiplier on outbound traffic for the whole run, borne by every third-party host in the user's bookmark collection. On a 2,000-bookmark account that is ~8,000 requests instead of ~2,000, materially raising the chance of tripping a remote host's rate limiting or bot protection — which, per the 4xx-means-delete rule, converts into deletions. The HTTP-status statistics are inflated by the same factor and cannot be read as a bookmark count.</impact>
<parameter name="fix">Validate only when the URL actually changed, and reuse the redirect probe's own response instead of re-requesting.

```go
func expandAndCheckURL(rawURL string, verbose bool) (string, error) {
	current := rawURL

	for _, step := range []struct {
		name string
		fn   func(string, bool) (string, error)
	}{
		{"expanded URL", expandURL},
		{"fixed URL", fixParenthesesSuffix},
		{"redirected URL", urlRedirects},
	} {
		next, err := step.fn(current, verbose)
		if err != nil {
			return "", err
		}
		// Only re-check when the step actually rewrote the URL; otherwise the
		// previous iteration already validated this exact URL.
		if next != current {
			if err := validateURLAccessibility(next, step.name); err != nil {
				return "", err
			}
		}
		current = next
	}

	return current, nil
}
```

This still leaves the initial URL unvalidated when nothing changes, so validate `rawURL` once before the loop. Net result: one `HEAD` for the common case, plus one per actual transformation.

Going further, `urlRedirects` already performs a `HEAD` and holds the response; having it return the status code alongside the URL would let the caller drop the separate validation entirely, taking the common case to a single request.</fix>
</invoke>

## Evidence

## Impact

## Fix

## Resolution
expandAndCheckURL validates the incoming URL once, then re-validates only after a step actually rewrote it, and rejects a rewritten URL that is not http(s). The common no-change path drops from four requests to two (one validation plus the redirect probe). Covered by TestExpandAndCheckURL_DoesNotRevalidateUnchangedURL, which counts the requests a recordingServer received and fails above two.

## Problem

`extractTitleFromHTML` returns `node.FirstChild.Data` — the first child node of `<title>`, not its concatenated text. A title interrupted by an entity reference or a comment is parsed into several sibling text nodes, and only the first is returned.

The walk also matches on element name alone with no namespace or ancestor check, so an SVG `<title>` (the accessible-name element inside `<svg>`) is accepted as the document title when it appears before the real one.</problem>
<parameter name="evidence">main.go:501-517:

```go
func extractTitleFromHTML(node *html.Node) string {
	if node.Type == html.ElementNode && node.Data == "title" {
		if node.FirstChild != nil {
			return node.FirstChild.Data
		}

		return ""
	}

	for child := node.FirstChild; child != nil; child = child.NextSibling {
		if title := extractTitleFromHTML(child); title != "" {
			return title
		}
	}

	return ""
}
```

Two gaps:

1. `node.FirstChild.Data` is one text node. `golang.org/x/net/html` splits `<title>A &amp; B</title>` into text nodes around the entity, so this returns `A ` rather than `A & B`.
2. There is no check that the element is in the HTML namespace or that its parent is `<head>`. `html.Node` carries `Namespace`, which is `"svg"` for elements inside an `<svg>` subtree; an inline SVG icon in the body with `<title>Search icon</title>` matches.

The existing tests use only single-text-node titles in `<head>` — main_test.go:106, :126, :145, :165 — so neither case is exercised:

```go
htmlContent := `<html><head><title>Test Page Title</title></head><body></body></html>`
```

`getPageTitle` reports 78.6% coverage and `extractTitleFromHTML` 100%, yet both gaps survive.</evidence>
<parameter name="impact">Bookmarks whose page title contains an entity get a truncated title written to Pinboard — and because `fetchTitleIfNeeded` only runs when the bookmark has no title at all (main.go:170), the truncated value is what the bookmark keeps. Pages with an inline SVG before the head title, or with `<title>` reachable earlier in document order, get an icon label as their bookmark title. Both are wrong values written to the user's account rather than a skipped update.</impact>
<parameter name="fix">Concatenate the text children and restrict the match to an HTML-namespace `<title>`:

```go
// extractTitleFromHTML returns the document title. It concatenates every text
// child rather than returning the first: x/net/html splits a title containing
// an entity reference into several sibling text nodes, so FirstChild alone
// truncates at the first entity. The namespace check skips <title> elements
// inside inline SVG, which are icon labels rather than the page title.
func extractTitleFromHTML(node *html.Node) string {
	if node.Type == html.ElementNode && node.Data == "title" && node.Namespace == "" {
		var sb strings.Builder
		for child := node.FirstChild; child != nil; child = child.NextSibling {
			if child.Type == html.TextNode {
				sb.WriteString(child.Data)
			}
		}

		return sb.String()
	}

	for child := node.FirstChild; child != nil; child = child.NextSibling {
		if title := extractTitleFromHTML(child); title != "" {
			return title
		}
	}

	return ""
}
```

Add test cases for `<title>A &amp; B</title>` expecting `A & B`, and for a document whose `<body>` contains `<svg><title>icon</title></svg>` before a real head title, expecting the head title.

Clamp the result to Pinboard's 255-character `title` limit at the `getPageTitle` boundary, per the unbounded-body finding.</fix>
</invoke>

## Evidence

## Impact

## Fix

## Resolution
extractTitleFromHTML now concatenates every text child of <title> instead of returning FirstChild.Data, and matches only elements with an empty Namespace so an inline SVG <title> is skipped. Covered by TestGetPageTitle cases "joins text split by an entity" (`A &amp; B` yields `A & B`) and "skips a title inside inline SVG". Title length is clamped to Pinboard's 255-rune limit in getPageTitle.

## Problem

`expandAndCheckURL` validates `urlString` before running any transformation. A URL that is broken *because* of a trailing parenthesis returns 4xx on that first check, so the function returns `errDeadLink` and `processBookmark` schedules a deletion — before `fixParenthesesSuffix` ever runs.

The repair steps exist precisely to turn a URL that fails now into one that works. Checking the input first makes them unreachable for every URL they were written to fix.

This is a regression introduced by the previous round's fix for `audit-467363cc`, which added the upfront validation to avoid re-validating an unchanged URL three times. The original code validated only *after* each step.</problem>
<parameter name="evidence">main.go:969-975:

```go
func expandAndCheckURL(urlString string, verbose bool) (string, error) {
	if err := validateURLAccessibility(urlString, "URL"); err != nil {
		return "", err
	}

	steps := []urlStep{
		{name: "expanded URL", fn: expandURL},
		{name: "fixed URL", fn: fixParenthesesSuffix, selfValidating: true},
		{name: "redirected URL", fn: urlRedirects},
	}
```

Demonstrated by a test written against a server that 404s any path ending in `)` and serves 200 otherwise — the exact shape the feature targets:

```
--- FAIL: TestExpandAndCheckURL_ParenthesisFixIsNotRevalidated
    expandAndCheckURL() error = dead link: URL returns status 404, want nil
```

The bookmark is never repaired; it is reported as a dead link, and main.go:456-470 turns that into `ActionDelete`.

The same reasoning applies to `expandURL`: a shortener whose short URL 404s directly would be deleted rather than expanded, though in practice shorteners answer with a redirect rather than a 4xx.

README.md advertises the behaviour this breaks:

```markdown
- Fixing URLs with trailing parentheses
```

and lists it under How It Works as a step that runs before the accessibility verdict.</evidence>
<parameter name="impact">Every bookmark whose URL was mangled with a trailing `)` — the Markdown copy-paste case the feature was written for — is deleted instead of repaired on a non-dry-run pass. This is silent, unrecoverable data loss on exactly the bookmarks the tool claims to fix, and it is worse than the pre-audit behaviour, which repaired them correctly.

It also means the parenthesis-fix statistics counter can only ever be incremented for URLs that already worked with the parenthesis attached, which is the case where the fix is pointless.</impact>
<parameter name="fix">Run the transformations first and validate once at the end, which both restores the repair and reduces the common path to the same request count:

```go
func expandAndCheckURL(urlString string, verbose bool) (string, error) {
	steps := []urlStep{
		{name: "expanded URL", fn: expandURL},
		{name: "fixed URL", fn: fixParenthesesSuffix},
		{name: "redirected URL", fn: urlRedirects},
	}

	current := urlString
	for _, step := range steps {
		next, err := step.fn(current, verbose)
		if err != nil {
			return "", err
		}
		if next == current {
			continue
		}
		if !isCheckableURL(next) {
			return "", fmt.Errorf("%w: %s is not an http(s) URL: %q",
				errUnverifiable, step.name, next)
		}
		current = next
	}

	// Validated once, after the repairs. Checking the input first made a URL
	// that fails only because of its trailing parenthesis look like a dead
	// link, so the bookmark was deleted instead of fixed.
	if err := validateURLAccessibility(current, "URL"); err != nil {
		return "", err
	}

	return current, nil
}
```

The `selfValidating` field on `urlStep` becomes unnecessary and should be removed with it; there is now exactly one validation and nothing to skip.

Keep the test that provoked this as the regression guard: a server answering 404 for paths ending in `)` and 200 otherwise, asserting `expandAndCheckURL` returns the trimmed URL and no error. Keep `TestExpandAndCheckURL_DoesNotRevalidateUnchangedURL` too — the restructure must not reintroduce the redundant checks that motivated the original change.</fix>
</invoke>

## Evidence

## Impact

## Fix

## Resolution
expandAndCheckURL no longer validates the input URL before the repair steps. expandURL and fixParenthesesSuffix run first, then followRedirects checks liveness at each hop, so a URL broken only by its trailing parenthesis is repaired instead of deleted. The selfValidating field on urlStep was removed with the restructure. Found by the test written for the LOW double-HEAD finding, which failed with "dead link: URL returns status 404" against a server that 404s any path ending in ")" — kept as TestExpandAndCheckURL_ParenthesisFixIsNotRevalidated. TestExpandAndCheckURL_DoesNotRevalidateUnchangedURL still holds, so the redundant-request fix this regressed from is not reintroduced.

## Problem

Every Pinboard call repeats an identical four-line sequence: `pinboardGet`, error return, `defer closeBody`, `checkPinboardStatus`. `updateBookmark` and `deleteBookmark` then both end with `return decodePinboardResult(resp)`, making them the same function apart from the parameters they build.

The consequence is not cosmetic: the previous audit found that a missing `format=json` at one of these sites broke auto-tagging entirely, and that a missing result-code check at two of them counted failed writes as successes. Both were single-site omissions in copies of a shared shape.</problem>
<parameter name="evidence">The preamble, repeated verbatim at four sites (main.go:670-678, 727-735, 744-752, 851-861):

```go
	resp, err := pinboardGet(path, params, apiToken)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	defer closeBody(resp.Body)

	if err := checkPinboardStatus(resp, path); err != nil {
		return err
	}
```

`grep -n 'checkPinboardStatus(resp, path)' main.go` → 676, 733, 750, 859.

`updateBookmark` (main.go:727-737) and `deleteBookmark` (main.go:744-754) are then byte-identical after their parameter construction:

```go
	resp, err := pinboardGet(path, params, apiToken)
	...
	return decodePinboardResult(resp)
```

The only difference is `params`: `url.Values{paramURL: {urlStr}}` versus the seven-key map built at main.go:710-725.

A related pair sits in `fetchTitleIfNeeded` and `fetchTagsIfNeeded` (main.go:438-484), which share their entire structure — skip guard, target-URL selection, fetch, record, verbose log, return — and duplicate this exact block (main.go:443-446 and 467-470):

```go
	urlToFetch := b.URL
	if newURL != "" {
		urlToFetch = newURL
	}
```

`dupl` is enabled in `.golangci.yml` but its default threshold (150 tokens) is above these blocks, so none of it is reported.</evidence>
<parameter name="impact">Four copies of the request preamble mean any future cross-cutting requirement — a retry policy, a header, request logging, a second status code to special-case — must be applied four times, and the previous audit shows that one-of-four omissions here produce silent, hard-to-detect defects rather than compile errors. The `updateBookmark`/`deleteBookmark` duplication additionally means a fix to the write path can be applied to one and missed on the other.</impact>
<parameter name="fix">Introduce one helper for writes and one for decoded reads.

```go
// pinboardWrite performs a Pinboard write and reports whether it succeeded.
// posts/add and posts/delete answer a rejected write in the body at HTTP 200,
// so both the status and the result code must be checked; keeping that in one
// place is why neither caller can omit half of it.
func pinboardWrite(path string, params url.Values, apiToken string) error {
	resp, err := pinboardGet(path, params, apiToken)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	defer closeBody(resp.Body)

	if err := checkPinboardStatus(resp, path); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	return decodePinboardResult(resp)
}
```

`deleteBookmark` collapses to one line; `updateBookmark` keeps only its parameter construction.

For the two decoding reads, add:

```go
// pinboardDecode performs a Pinboard read and decodes the JSON body into out.
func pinboardDecode(path string, params url.Values, apiToken string, out any) error {
	resp, err := pinboardGet(path, params, apiToken)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	defer closeBody(resp.Body)

	if statusErr := checkPinboardStatus(resp, path); statusErr != nil {
		return statusErr
	}

	if err := json.NewDecoder(resp.Body).Decode(out); err != nil {
		return fmt.Errorf("failed to decode %s response: %w", path, err)
	}

	return nil
}
```

`getSuggestedTags` must keep its `stats.RecordHTTPStatus(opTagFetch, ...)` call, so either pass a status hook or record it inside the helper keyed by path — the former is clearer.

For the fetch pair, extract the shared target selection:

```go
// targetURL prefers the rewritten URL when expansion produced one.
func targetURL(original, rewritten string) string {
	if rewritten != "" {
		return rewritten
	}

	return original
}
```

Leave `fetchTitleIfNeeded` and `fetchTagsIfNeeded` as separate functions — their skip conditions and stats calls differ — but have both use `targetURL`.</fix>
</invoke>

## Evidence

## Impact

## Fix

## Resolution
Added pinboardWrite (pinboardGet + status check + result-code check) and pinboardDecode (pinboardGet + optional status recording + status check + JSON decode). deleteBookmark collapsed to a single line; updateBookmark keeps only its parameter construction; getBookmarks and getSuggestedTags each lost their preamble. The four copies of the request preamble are now one, so a cross-cutting change cannot be applied to three of four sites — the failure mode that produced both the missing format=json and the missing result-code check. Also extracted targetURL, removing the duplicated newURL-selection block from fetchTitleIfNeeded and fetchTagsIfNeeded; covered by TestTargetURL.

## Problem

`printTitleStats` and `printTagStats` differ only in two field names and a label. `printShortenerStats` contains the same six-line block three times, once per shortener, with the service key hardcoded in five places each.

Both are also among the least-covered functions in the package, because the duplicated branches multiply the cases a test has to enumerate.</problem>
<parameter name="evidence">statistics.go:340-362 — the two functions side by side:

```go
func (s *Statistics) printTitleStats() {
	if s.TitlesFetched == 0 && s.TitlesFailed == 0 {
		return
	}

	fmt.Printf("   Titles fetched:     %d", s.TitlesFetched)
	if s.TitlesFailed > 0 {
		fmt.Printf(" (%d failed)", s.TitlesFailed)
	}
	fmt.Println()
}

func (s *Statistics) printTagStats() {
	if s.TagsFetched == 0 && s.TagsFailed == 0 {
		return
	}

	fmt.Printf("   Tags fetched:       %d", s.TagsFetched)
	if s.TagsFailed > 0 {
		fmt.Printf(" (%d failed)", s.TagsFailed)
	}
	fmt.Println()
}
```

statistics.go:264-282 — the shortener block, three times:

```go
	if s.ShortURLsExpanded["bitly"] > 0 || s.ShortURLsFailed["bitly"] > 0 {
		fmt.Printf("     • bit.ly:         %d success, %d failed\n",
			s.ShortURLsExpanded["bitly"], s.ShortURLsFailed["bitly"])
	}
	if s.ShortURLsExpanded["tinyurl"] > 0 || s.ShortURLsFailed["tinyurl"] > 0 { ... }
	if s.ShortURLsExpanded["isgd"] > 0 || s.ShortURLsFailed["isgd"] > 0 { ... }
```

The service keys are string literals here, while `expandURL` (main.go:1084-1093) writes them from its own literals — so adding a shortener requires edits in two files with no compiler link between them.

Coverage on the affected functions:

```
statistics.go:264:  printShortenerStats  88.9%
statistics.go:340:  printTitleStats      66.7%
statistics.go:352:  printTagStats        33.3%
```

`printTagStats` at 33.3% means only its early return is exercised.</evidence>
<parameter name="impact">A change to the fetch-statistics format has to be made twice and will be made once. Adding a fourth shortener requires remembering an unlinked second edit site in another file, and the tool would silently omit it from the report — the same silent-omission shape as the duplicated Pinboard preamble. The duplicated branches also inflate the number of test cases needed for full coverage, which is why two of these three functions sit below 70%.</impact>
<parameter name="fix">Collapse the fetch pair into one parameterised method:

```go
// printFetchStats renders one "N fetched (M failed)" line, or nothing when the
// operation never ran.
func printFetchStats(label string, fetched, failed int) {
	if fetched == 0 && failed == 0 {
		return
	}

	fmt.Printf("   %-20s%d", label+":", fetched)
	if failed > 0 {
		fmt.Printf(" (%d failed)", failed)
